    "model": "(i) In distilled water, a red blood cell takes in water by osmosis, swells and may burst; the bursting is haemolysis. A plant cell takes in water into its vacuole by osmosis. Its cytoplasm is pushed against the cell wall, which resists further swelling, so the cell becomes turgid and does not burst. (ii) In a concentrated salt solution, a red blood cell loses water by osmosis and shrinks, becoming shrivelled and spiky; this is crenation. A plant cell loses water from its vacuole, becomes flaccid, and its cytoplasm and cell membrane shrink away from the cell wall; this is plasmolysis. The space between the wall and the membrane fills with the outside solution, because the cell wall is fully permeable. The cell wall is what stops the plant cell from bursting in water.",
