    "model": "Water enters the root hairs from the soil by osmosis, because the cell sap is more concentrated than the soil water. It moves across the cortex from cell to cell by osmosis, passes through the endodermis and enters the root xylem. Root pressure is produced when living root cells actively pump mineral ions into the xylem, so that water enters by osmosis and is pushed a short distance up the stem from below. It is far too weak to raise water to the top of a tall tree. The main mechanism is the cohesion–tension model, proposed by Dixon and Joly in 1894. Water evaporates from the mesophyll cells and diffuses out through the stomata, so the water column in the xylem is under tension and is pulled up from above. Cohesion, the attraction between water molecules by hydrogen bonds, keeps the column unbroken, and adhesion to the walls of the xylem vessels helps to hold it up. The process is passive, powered by the sun's energy, and the lignified walls of the xylem stop the vessels collapsing.",
